    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Benin City was the seat of the Ọba, whose line began, according to tradition, when a son of Ọranmiyan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> was set on the throne, and it grew rich in the 16th and 17th centuries on trade with Europeans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>.</li><li>On 17 February 1897 a British force of 1,200 men captured, sacked and burnt the city and took the bronzes from the palace, an act that opened the way to British colonisation.</li><li>The British allowed the monarchy to be restored in 1914, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>'s independence the city became the capital of the Mid-Western Region, of Bendel State and, from 1991, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1691 a Portuguese captain wrote that Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> was larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and that the people had no doors to their houses; in 1701 or 1702 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchant called the same city a &quot;mere village&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

