    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1290</span><span class="rf-text">Ọba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewedo">Ewedo</a> begins the imperial expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1470</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> changes the name of the state to Ẹdo</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1485</span><span class="rf-text">The Portuguese visit Benin City</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1701</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchant describes the city as a mere village of half-ruined houses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1897</span><span class="rf-text">A British force captures, sacks and burns Benin City and takes the bronzes from the palace</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1914</span><span class="rf-text">The British permit the restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> monarchy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1963</span><span class="rf-text">Benin City becomes the capital of the Mid-Western Region</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1991</span><span class="rf-text">Benin City becomes the capital of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Benin City stands in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, in a climate that is hot and humid through the year. Its indigenous people are the Edo, also called the Bini, who speak the Edo language. The city's early history is known chiefly through tradition. According to tradition, the first rulers of the Edo were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogiso</a>, the "kings of the sky", who called the land Igodomigodo. Igodo was the first Ogiso and was succeeded by his eldest son Ere. A struggle for power followed between the wife of the last Ogiso and his son, Prince Ekaladerhan, who was framed by her and sentenced to death. The men sent to kill him released him at Ughoton, and he went into exile and took a new name.</p><p>When the elders, led by Chief Oliha, searched for the banished prince, he refused to return and sent his son Ọranmiyan in his place. Ọranmiyan took up residence in a palace built for him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzama">Uzama</a>, now a coronation shrine. He married Ẹrinmwide, daughter of the ninth Enogie of Egor, and they had a son. Ọranmiyan renounced his office, calling the land Ile-Ibinu, a land of vexation, and had his son made king. Ọba Ẹwẹka later renamed the capital Ubinu, a name the Portuguese reinterpreted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> tradition tells the story differently, but both the Yoruba and the Edo agree that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> sent Ọranmiyan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> to rule Benin City and that he founded the Ọba dynasty there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> began in the last decade of the 13th century under Ọba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewedo">Ewedo</a>. Around 1470 Ẹwuare changed the name of the state to Ẹdo, about the time the people of Ọkpẹkpẹ migrated from the city. The Portuguese visited Benin City around 1485, and the city grew rich during the 16th and 17th centuries through trade within southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and with Europeans, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. In the early 16th century the Ọba sent an ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and the King of Portugal sent Christian missionaries to Benin.</p><p>European visitors compared the city to their own. A Portuguese captain, Lourenco Pinto, described it in 1691: "Great Benin, where the king resides, is larger than Lisbon; all the streets run straight and as far as the eye can see." He wrote that the king's house was large and richly decorated, with fine columns, that the city was wealthy and industrious, and that it was so well governed that theft was unknown and the people had no doors to their houses. The people of the region also farmed yam, plantain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> for subsistence. A large rampart and ditch, the Benin city wall or moat, surrounded the city, and the inhabited town of the 19th century lay about a mile beyond it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-rawson.jpg" alt="Harry Rawson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson">Harry Rawson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Admiral_Sir_Harry_Rawson,_Sydney,_c._1902.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Freeman Brothers Studio, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to tradition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogiso dynasty</a> ruled the land first, and the Ọba line began when Ọranmiyan's son became king. In one account Ọranmiyan's son Ajaka ultimately became the first Ọba of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> of the present line, while Ọranmiyan himself reigned as Ọọni of Ifẹ. Ẹwẹka I was an Ọba of Benin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewedo">Ewedo</a> began the expansion of the state in the last decade of the 13th century. Ẹwuare changed the state's name to Ẹdo around 1470.</p><p>On 17 February 1897 the city fell to the British. The expedition's force of 1,200 men was under Admiral Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson">Harry Rawson</a>. The capture of Benin opened the way to British colonisation, and the kingdom was merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> Coast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>, then the Protectorate of Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and finally the Colony and Protectorate of Nigeria. The British permitted the restoration of the Benin monarchy in 1914, but true political power still lay with the colonial administration of Nigeria.</p><p>After Nigeria's independence in 1960, Benin City became the capital of the Mid-Western Region when that region was split from Western Region in June 1963. In 1976 the region was renamed Bendel State, and the city remained its capital. It became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a> in 1991, when Bendel State was divided into Delta State and Edo State.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes.jpg" alt="Benin Bronzes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benin_brass_plaque_03_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Edo are the indigenous people of Benin City. They have one of the richest dress cultures on the African continent and are known for their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, which stand for royalty and stand out at traditional marriages, as well as for body marks, bangles, anklets and raffia work. The Bini have four market days: Ekioba, Ekenaka, Agbado and Eken.</p><p>The Binis are known for bronze sculpture and casting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igun-street">Igun Street</a> in the city is known for bronze casting and other metal work. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> were portrait figures, busts and groups made in iron, carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and especially in brass, which is conventionally called bronze. Contact with the Portuguese left its mark on language: some residents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> could still speak a pidgin Portuguese in the late 19th century, and many Portuguese loan words are still found in the languages of the area.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igue-festival">Igue festival</a> is held each year to usher in the new year and is believed to renew Ọba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a>'s magical powers; it comes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and New Year celebrations and includes the Ọba's blessing of the land and his people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">Human sacrifice</a> was reported in the city by Alan Boisragon, a survivor of the Benin Massacre, in an account of 1898. The historian James D. Graham notes that there is little doubt that sacrifice was an integral part of the Benin state religion from very early days, but that first-hand accounts varied significantly, some reporting it and others making no mention of it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the 18th and 19th centuries visitors found the city smaller than before. In a letter of 1701 or 1702 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> merchant David van Nyendael described Benin City as a "mere village". He praised the king's palace, with its turreted roofs decorated with brass snakes, and called the intact houses "large and handsome", but noted that the town had clearly been more densely populated in the past and was now mostly "half-ruined houses". In 1820 a British naval officer, Lieutenant John King, wrote that the walls had been largely destroyed and that the city had formerly been depopulated by a civil war, so that its inhabited area did not exceed two or three miles in circumference.</p><p>In 1897 a British delegation led by Acting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-robert-phillips">James Robert Phillips</a> was ambushed, and all but two of its men were killed. The British answered with what they called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-expedition-of-1897">Punitive Expedition</a>. A force of 1,200 men under Admiral Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson">Harry Rawson</a> captured Benin City on 17 February 1897, sacked it and burnt it. Reginald Bacon, a member of the expedition, described the town that he found as an irregular straggling town of groups of houses separated by patches of bush, about a mile and a half from east to west and a mile from north to south. The force looted the palace. Some of the bronzes were auctioned to cover the expenses of the capture and occupation of the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/idia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idia.jpg" alt="Idia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/idia">Idia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memorial_bust_of_a_king's_mother_iyoba,_Nigeria,_Benin_Kingdom,_early_16th_century_AD,_gunmetal_bronze_-_Ethnological_Museum,_Berlin_-_DSC02225_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daderot, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> were looted from the palace by the expeditionary force. Most of them are today in western museums at locations around the world, and in recent years appeals have gone to various museums and governments to return them. The best-known of them, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idia">Queen Idia</a> mask, was used as the mascot of the Second Festival of Arts and Culture (FESTAC '77), held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> in 1977, and is now known as the Festac Mask.</p><p>Benin City is the home of one of the oldest ongoing monarchies in the world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>'s palace, the National Museum, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> moats, King's Square and the city's traditional markets mark the old city. The Benin moats measure about 20 to 40 feet.</p><p>Benin City is today the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a> in southern Nigeria. By the 2006 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> it was the fourth-largest city in Nigeria, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a>. It is one of the principal commercial and industrial centres in the south of the country and the centre of Nigeria's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> industry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and palm kernels are significant exports. The University of Benin is among its institutions of higher learning.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Benin City, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, was the seat of the Ọba of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and grew rich on trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> with the Portuguese. A British force burnt it and took its bronzes in 1897. It is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>.</em></p>

